.\" karvi(1): karvi's manual page, what every word shares. Written by hand
.\" except the regions between the markers, which tools/mangen rewrites from
.\" the top help text and the exit statuses (make generate).
.TH KARVI 1 "" "karvi"
.\" Paths and options are not hyphenated, and lines are not stretched.
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
karvi \- run the fleet, gather the output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" BEGIN GENERATED SYNOPSIS: tools/mangen from internal/cli; edit there
\fBkarvi\fR [global\-options] \fI<command>\fR [options] [payload]
.\" END GENERATED SYNOPSIS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B karvi
runs commands on network devices over SSH and keeps what they answer.
.B login
opens one interactive session;
.B command
runs commands on one device;
.B run
runs them across a set of devices, from the inventory or named directly,
one at a time, in a fixed pool, or in waves;
.B crun
collects them into one file per device; and
.B stream
reads runs line by line from standard input.
A run goes to the operator's private daemon, which holds the job and its
records, unless
.B \-\-no\-daemon
runs it in the client;
.B job
follows or cancels it, and
.B watch
shows every job on the host.
.PP
Each command word has its own page,
.BR karvi\-WORD (1),
which
.B man karvi WORD
finds; this page states what every word shares.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED DESCRIPTION: tools/mangen from internal/cli; edit there
.SS "Commands"
.TP
\fBlogin\fR
Open one interactive SSH session
.TP
\fBcommand\fR, \fBcmd\fR
Run commands on one device
.TP
\fBrun\fR
Run commands against an explicit scope
.TP
\fBcrun\fR
Collect commands from a scope into one file per device
.TP
\fBstream\fR, \fB\-\fR
Read a run line by line from standard input
.TP
\fBdaemon\fR \fBstart|stop|restart|status|serve\fR
Manage the private per\-user daemon
.TP
\fBjob\fR \fBfollow|cancel\fR
Follow or cancel one accepted job by its ID
.TP
\fBconfig\fR \fBgenerate|validate|show|colors\fR
Configuration tools
.TP
\fBsetup\fR \fBshared|tab\fR
Site preparations as root: the shared trees, tab completion
.TP
\fBwatch\fR
Render safe activity scoreboards
.TP
\fBversion\fR
Print build, schema, and SSH transport versions
.SS "Global options (before the command word)"
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR, \fB\-\-cfg\fR \fIPATH\fR
Add a config root (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-set\fR \fIKEY=VALUE\fR
Highest\-precedence override (repeatable, lock\-aware)
.TP
\fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress routine narration
.TP
\fB\-\-debug\fR
Safe provenance diagnostics
.TP
\fB\-\-debug\-show\-secrets\fR
Console\-only bounded hints; requires \-\-debug
.TP
\fB\-\-timezone\fR \fIZONE\fR
Override timezone
.TP
\fB\-\-ansi\fR \fIauto|strip|preserve\fR
Device stdout ANSI behavior
.TP
\fB\-\-ipv4\fR, \fB\-\-4\fR
Prefer IPv4 when both families are available
.TP
\fB\-\-ipv6\fR, \fB\-\-6\fR
Prefer IPv6 when both families are available
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print version without creating state
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR, \fB\-\-h\fR
Print this help without creating state
.PP
Every command word, subcommand word, and option may be abbreviated to any
prefix that names exactly one word valid at that position; a full name or
alias always matches exactly, so \-\-t is \-\-target and \-\-h is \-\-help. Options
take one or two leading dashes (\-echo and \-\-echo are the same), and a value
may be attached with =. In command and run, options are recognized only until
device command text begins; after that every argument, including \-h and \-\-,
is sent to the device. Use \-\- to begin device text with a dash.
.PP
An option that stands for a configuration key, such as \-\-order for
dispatch.order or \-\-workers for dispatch.parallel\-workers, sets that key for
the invocation, above the files and the environment and below \-\-set: a key
the site has locked refuses the option, and the key's range applies.
.\" END GENERATED DESCRIPTION
.SH CONFIGURATION
karvi reads its configuration in layers, each later one setting what an
earlier one set:
.IP 1. 4
the built\-in defaults;
.IP 2. 4
the global file,
.I /etc/karvi/config.toml
or, when it does not exist,
.IR /opt/karvi/config.toml ,
the only place a lock may be declared
.RB ( [config\-lock] );
.IP 3. 4
the operator's file,
.IR \(ti/.config/karvi/config.toml ,
unless the global file sets
.B config.allow\-user\-layer
to
.BR false ;
.IP 4. 4
each
.B \-\-config
.IR PATH ,
in order: a file, or a directory whose
.I .toml
files are read;
.IP 5. 4
the environment,
.B KARVI__SECTION__KEY
(see ENVIRONMENT);
.IP 6. 4
the options that stand for a key, such as
.B \-\-order
for
.B dispatch.order
or
.B \-\-workers
for
.BR dispatch.parallel\-workers ;
.IP 7. 4
.B \-\-set
.IR KEY = VALUE ,
in order.
.PP
A key the global file locks refuses every later layer's write, an option's
included, with
.B config_lock_violation
(exit 3), even a write of the value it holds.
A file may include others
.RB ( config.includes ).
.B karvi config show
.I KEY ...
prints each key's value, its source, its default, its range, its lock, and
the layers it overrode, and, for a key whose value is a place karvi writes
or reads, a
.B resolved:
line, the path the next activity would use, found without creating
anything, with a
.B passed:
line for each candidate on the host passed by;
.B karvi config validate
checks a file;
.B karvi config generate
writes one.
The home in
.I \(ti
is the password database's, so
.B HOME
moves neither the operator's file nor the private root;
.I \(tiUSER
is refused, and a relative path in a place or file key is taken from the
working directory.
.SH VALUES
.TP
.I DURATION
A number and its unit,
.BR h ,
.BR m ,
.BR s ,
.BR ms ,
.BR us ,
or
.BR ns ,
such as
.BR 500ms ,
.BR 45s ,
or
.BR 5m ;
the parts combine
.RB ( 1h30m ).
A bare number and a
.B d
are refused.
The key behind the option sets the range
.RB ( \-\-wave\-delay
at most
.BR 1h ),
which
.B karvi config show
.I KEY
.B \-\-explain
prints.
.TP
.I N
A whole number; what 0 means is each option's own, as its entry says
("0 off", "0 the host's logical CPUs").
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KARVI__SECTION__KEY
Sets a configuration key, above the files and below the options and
.BR \-\-set :
the key's dots as two underscores and its hyphens as one, in capitals
.RB ( KARVI__DISPATCH__ORDER
sets
.BR dispatch.order ,
.B KARVI__SPOOLDIR
sets
.BR spooldir ).
An unknown name is refused unless
.B config.reject\-unknown\-env
is
.BR false .
.TP
.BR NETUSER ", " NETPASS ", " NETENABLE
The device username, password, and enable password of the credential
fallback where the platform's
.B fallback
holds
.B netvars
(the network built\-ins; a
.B linux
server takes the operator's keys,
.BR ssh.identities ),
read by the client that submits the job and never by the daemon; one that
is not set is asked at the terminal where the fallback holds
.BR prompt .
.TP
.B KARVI_ASKPASS_PATH
The
.BR karvi\-askpass (1)
helper, before the one beside the executable and the one on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B COLORFGBG
Under
.B display.theme
.BR auto ,
a last field of 7 or more takes the light theme, anything else the dark.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set and not empty, karvi adds no colour under
.B display.color
.BR auto ,
the default, on any word and in
.BR karvi\-prune (8)'s
help;
.B always
and
.BR never ,
and the watch screen's
.BR \-\-color ,
are explicit and it does not override them.
.TP
.B SUDO_GID
.B sudo karvi setup shared
takes its group as the trees' group when
.B \-\-group
is not given.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/karvi/config.toml\fR, \fI/opt/karvi/config.toml
The global configuration, the first that exists.
.TP
.I \(ti/.config/karvi/config.toml
The operator's configuration.
.TP
.I /opt/karvi/users/USER\fR, \fI/var/lib/karvi/users/USER
The operator's private root
.RB ( basedir ),
where the site made the
.I users
directory
.RB ( "sudo karvi setup shared" ).
.TP
.I \(ti/.local/share/karvi
The operator's private root otherwise.
Under the root:
.I jobs
and
.I transcripts
(where no shared tree exists),
.I crun
(the collection directory, likewise),
.IR socket/d.sock " and " socket/c.sock
(the daemon's sockets),
.I state/daemon.json
and
.I logs/daemon.log
(the daemon's state and log).
.TP
.I BASEDIR/known_hosts
The trust store
.RB ( ssh.known\-hosts\-file
.BR auto ),
in the operator's private root above;
.B karvi config show \-\-explain ssh.known\-hosts\-file
names it.
.TP
.I /opt/karvi/shared\fR, \fI/var/lib/karvi/shared
The shared trees,
.IR jobs ,
.IR crun ,
and
.IR transcripts ,
in the operators' group: the defaults of
.BR output.root ,
.BR crun.directory ,
and
.B transcript.root
where they exist.
.TP
.I /dev/shm/karvi
The scratch root, made by
.B "sudo karvi setup shared"
and never by an operator's run:
.I scoreboards
(the
.B scoreboards
key, what
.B karvi watch
reads),
.I capacity
(the host's session ledger), and each operator's scratch and control
sockets; where it is absent, each is under the private root
.RI ( BASEDIR/state/scoreboards ,
.IR BASEDIR/state/capacity ).
.B karvi config show \-\-explain
names every place on its
.B resolved:
lines.
.TP
.I /tmp/karvi\-UID\fR, \fI/var/tmp/karvi\-UID
The spool directory, the first that can be written
.RB ( spooldir
.BR auto ).
.TP
.I /etc/tmpfiles.d/karvi.conf\fR, \fI/etc/bash_completion.d/karvi
Written by
.B sudo karvi setup shared
and
.BR "sudo karvi setup tab" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Loading the configuration exits 2, or 3 for a lock, whatever the cause.
A run (\fBrun\fR, \fBcrun\fR, a job of \fBstream\fR) exits 0 when every
device succeeded and 101 when any failed or did not start, every device
included, unless a halt or a gate stopped it; \fBlogin\fR and \fBcommand\fR,
on one device, exit with the device's failure (107 to 110).
Any word exits with an earlier stage's status when that stage fails: the
configuration, the target set (5), a credential (6), a name (7).
When more than one applies, the first of 111, 106, 113, 114, 102, 103, 104,
and 105 is the exit.
A failure's message begins with its error code, and the records carry it.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED EXIT STATUS: tools/mangen from internal/exitcode; edit there
.TP
.B 0
\fIExitSuccess\fR: Every device succeeded, or the command did what it was asked.
.TP
.B 1
\fIExitGenericError\fR: An internal failure with no more specific status.
.TP
.B 2
\fIExitConfigValidationError\fR: The configuration did not load: a file, a key, a value, or its range.
.TP
.B 3
\fIExitConfigLockViolation\fR: A site lock refused a write to a key: \-\-set, an option, the environment, or a lower file.
.TP
.B 4
\fIExitUsageError\fR: The command line was refused: an option, a value, a command, or a file it names.
.TP
.B 5
\fIExitInventoryError\fR: The target set could not be built: an inventory source, a target file, a row, or a platform is unreadable or invalid, or nothing was selected.
.TP
.B 6
\fIExitCredentialResolutionError\fR: No credential could be resolved for a device, or a credential source is invalid or unsafe.
.TP
.B 7
\fIExitNameResolutionError\fR: A device name did not resolve to an address of a usable family.
.TP
.B 8
\fIExitDependencyError\fR: A program, transport, or facility karvi needs is missing or would not start.
.TP
.B 9
\fIExitPermissionError\fR: A file or directory karvi must use is missing, unsafe, or not writable, or an action needs root or is not allowed.
.TP
.B 101
\fIExitPartialFailure\fR: A run in which one or more devices failed or did not start, no halt or gate applying.
.TP
.B 102
\fIExitHaltErrorCount\fR: A run stopped starting devices at its failure count (dispatch.halt\-on\-error\-count).
.TP
.B 103
\fIExitHaltErrorPercent\fR: A run stopped starting devices at its failure percent (dispatch.halt\-on\-error\-percent).
.TP
.B 104
\fIExitWaveGateErrorCount\fR: A wave job stopped between waves at the gate's count (dispatch.wave\-gate\-error\-count).
.TP
.B 105
\fIExitWaveGateErrorPercent\fR: A wave job stopped between waves at the gate's percent (dispatch.wave\-gate\-error\-percent).
.TP
.B 106
\fIExitShutdownIncomplete\fR: The daemon stopped before the job ended, or a stop did not finish in time.
.TP
.B 107
\fIExitDeviceFailure\fR: The device refused a command, a command exited with a failure status or a signal, a command or the device timed out, or the session's set\-up failed.
.TP
.B 108
\fIExitAuthenticationFailure\fR: The device refused the login.
.TP
.B 109
\fIExitHostKeyFailure\fR: The device's host key was refused, or the trust store could not be used.
.TP
.B 110
\fIExitConnectionFailure\fR: A connection to a device or to the daemon failed or was lost.
.TP
.B 111
\fIExitOutputFailure\fR: Output could not be written: a record, a file, the audit, or standard output.
.TP
.B 112
\fIExitJobRejected\fR: The daemon refused the request, or the exchange with it failed.
.TP
.B 113
\fIExitCancelled\fR: Cancelled: job cancel, Ctrl\-C, or an interrupted wait.
.TP
.B 114
\fIExitHaltHostKeyMismatch\fR: A run stopped starting devices after a changed host key (ssh.halt\-run\-on\-host\-key\-mismatch).
.\" END GENERATED EXIT STATUS
